-- | Bind a receipt chain to where it came from.
--
-- SPEC.md section 8 says the chain "is not signed. It proves internal
-- consistency, not origin. Anyone can produce a valid chain saying anything."
-- That is exactly true and it is the last hole in the evidence: hand someone a
-- completely fabricated receipts.js and the verifier goes green, because every
-- seal in it really does follow from the one before.
--
-- The fix SPEC.md names is to bind it to a ledger transaction, and that is
-- what this is. The principal publishes the final seal of a chain on Canton.
-- Forging a chain now means forging a Canton contract signed by a party whose
-- key you do not have, which is a different and much harder problem.
--
-- What this does NOT do, said here so nobody has to guess:
--   * It does not prove the receipts are true. It proves this exact chain was
--     published by this party at this time. A principal can anchor a chain of
--     lies; they just cannot anchor it and later deny it, or swap it for a
--     different one afterwards.
--   * It does not stop a chain being discarded entirely. Anchoring makes
--     substitution detectable, not deletion.
module KyaAnchor where

import DA.Text (explode)
import qualified DA.Text as T

-- explode gives [Text], one single-character Text per character, so the digit
-- set has to be exploded too rather than compared against a Text.
hexDigits : [Text]
hexDigits = explode "0123456789abcdef"

-- | One published chain head. Immutable by construction: there is no choice
-- that edits it, so a new chain means a new contract and the old one stays on
-- the ledger as history.
template ChainAnchor
  with
    principal : Party
      -- ^ Whose desk this chain belongs to. The only signatory: an anchor is
      -- a statement by the principal about their own records, and nobody
      -- else's signature makes it more true.
    seal      : Text
      -- ^ The final seal of the chain, 64 lowercase hex characters.
    receipts  : Int
      -- ^ How many receipts that seal covers. Without this, a chain could be
      -- truncated to an earlier receipt and re-anchored at that receipt's
      -- seal, which would verify. With it, the count has to match too.
    ledger    : Text
      -- ^ Which rail produced the chain, copied from the receipts. A MOCKED
      -- chain anchored on real Canton is still a MOCKED chain and must say so.
      --
      -- There is deliberately no anchoredAt field. The creator would supply
      -- it, which makes it a number they choose rather than a fact; Canton
      -- already records when the contract was created, and that record they
      -- cannot write. A timestamp inside the payload would look like evidence
      -- while being the one field in here nobody has checked.
  where
    signatory principal

    -- A seal is a sha256 in lowercase hex. Anything else is not a chain head,
    -- and anchoring one would put a permanent lie on the ledger.
    -- The label rule goes first deliberately. tests/mutation.py proves a fence
    -- matters by deleting its line and requiring a named test to go red, and
    -- the first term of an `ensure` chain cannot be deleted on its own without
    -- leaving a dangling `&&`. So the term that sits in the undeletable slot is
    -- the one about labelling, not one of the three about the seal itself.
    ensure ledger /= ""
        && T.length seal == 64
        && all (`elem` hexDigits) (explode seal)
        && receipts > 0

    -- Anyone holding the receipts file can be shown the anchor; that is the
    -- entire point of publishing it.
    choice Confirm : Bool
      with
        claimedSeal : Text
        claimedCount : Int
      controller principal
      do
        -- Both, not just the seal. A truncated chain has a real seal -- the
        -- seal of the receipt it was cut at -- so the count is what catches it.
        pure (claimedSeal == seal && claimedCount == receipts)

-- Publishing is a plain create. There was a two-step AnchorRequest/Publish
-- here and it earned nothing: the principal is the only signatory, so there is
-- no second party to agree, and the extra template moved the ensure clause off
-- the thing being validated -- a malformed request could be created and only
-- refused a step later. One template, validated at the door.
